WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.102
-Our next guest is
the National Book Award

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:05.138
winning author of her
critically acclaimed novels

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:07.773
"Salvage the Bones"
and "Sing, Unburied, Sing."

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:10.776
Her fantastic new novel,
"Let Us Descend,"

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:12.178
was published today.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.514
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Jesmyn Ward.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.282
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:29.229 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:31.463
Welcome back.
-It's good to be here.

00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:33.566
-I'm so happy to have you.
I asked you backstage --

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.200
are you relieved
once a book is written?

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.369
And you were very honest,
and you said you'd be relieved

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:38.938
when the press is over.
-Yes.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:42.675
-Well, thank you very much
for getting through it with us.

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:45.344
This -- I know people have
told you this over the years,

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:47.847
and it's something
that I believe, as well.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.748
Your characters stay with people

00:00:49.748 --> 00:00:51.951
long after
they've read your books.

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.252
Is that something --

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:54.888
Is that a compliment
that you enjoy hearing?

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.923
Is that something you're trying
to do when you're writing?

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:59.058
-Yes.

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:02.128
Yes, because I think

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:04.797
that literature
and that stories

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:07.600
act in a way that...

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.435
other mediums don't, right?

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:14.540
I think they allow readers to
sink into a world with a person

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.678
and to feel like they're living
that reality with that person.

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:21.514
And I think that the people
that I write about,

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:23.349
the characters
that I write about,

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:26.519
are worthy of
that kind of regard, so...

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.188
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:33.092
-I know -- When you were here
for your National Book Award

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:34.661
winning book,
"Sing, Unburied, Sing,"

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:35.895
that's 2017.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.530
You'd started this book.
-Yes.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.767
-And I know it was
a very difficult book to write,

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.568
and it's about
very difficult themes.

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:45.137
I also want to say
I found it very easy to read,

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:47.673
which is a credit
to the beautiful way you write.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:49.575
But can you talk about
getting over

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:53.346
that hurdle of addressing
sort of the themes

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:55.447
and the difficult histories
that you're addressing in this?

00:01:55.447 --> 00:01:58.618
-Right. This...

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.386
It was really difficult
to write this book

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:04.023
in part because
I'd never written about anyone

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:08.360
who didn't have a lot
of physical agency, right?

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.196
I mean, Annis is
the main character in here,

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.997
and she's very constrained

00:02:11.997 --> 00:02:14.733
because she's
an enslaved person, right?

00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:17.704
But in the writing of the book,

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:21.540
I began to discover that she has
other types of agency, right?

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.811
She has emotional agency.
She has imaginative agency.

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:27.947
She has the agency of memory.

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.249
She also has a type
of spiritual agency.

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:32.852
So I think that
all of these agencies combined

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:37.056
gave her a way
to move through the world

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.993
that was not confined, right.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:43.061
that was not constrained
by the system of slavery.

00:02:43.061 --> 00:02:44.397
And I think it made it --

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:47.800
Hopefully it will make it easier
for readers to read

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.935
because they can experience

00:02:49.935 --> 00:02:53.506
all of those freedoms
and agencies with her.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.008
-There is, you know,

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:57.343
obviously a great deal
of tragedy in this book,

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.778
a great deal of grief
and the processing of grief,

00:02:59.778 --> 00:03:01.747
but it's also
a very hopeful book.

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:05.251
When you're writing it,
do you know these are all things

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:07.720
that you want to make sure
exist all at once?

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.922
Like, there's no obviously
neat endings,

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:11.590
and it's not like
one section is about this,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.225
one section is about that.

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:14.594
Is it always important for you

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:17.663
to have it sort of
under the same umbrella?

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:19.766
-I think so. I mean...

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:23.803
You know, like, she -- I --

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:26.806
Annis is not a victim.
And she's also --

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:28.441
Because a victim --

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:30.342
When a person
is portrayed as a victim,

00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:32.311
they're flattened, right?

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:34.113
They're just victorious,
and that's it.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:39.418
But she's also not -- She's not
only a survivor either, right?

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:41.287
Because when someone
is only a survivor,

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:43.756
then their experience
is also flattened, right?

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:47.860
I wanted her to be
as complicated and complex

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.229
as I could possibly make her.

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:54.934
So she experiences friendship,
she experiences connection,

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:58.371
she has lovers, you know,
at the same time

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:02.274
that she also feels grief
and feels longing

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:06.011
and, you know, misses her
community and misses friends.

00:04:06.011 --> 00:04:09.549
So there's -- I want all of that
to exist at the same time

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:13.920
because I think that reflects
how complicated we all are.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.456
-When you write such a sort of
multi-dimensional character

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:21.593
like this that does have
this great wealth of humanity,

00:04:21.593 --> 00:04:23.128
do you find when you're writing

00:04:23.128 --> 00:04:25.665
the character is telling you
about themselves

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:27.900
or do you find
that you're putting yourself

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.968
into the character?

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:33.505
-I think there's a little bit
of both happening, right?

00:04:33.505 --> 00:04:36.642
Because whenever I write,

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:40.613
I always feel like the character
who is speaking

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:43.582
is sitting here
and telling me the story.

00:04:43.582 --> 00:04:45.884
So in part I'm channeling them.

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:50.523
But at the same time,
I'm also, I think,

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.591
pulling on my own experience

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:58.097
to try to understand some of
what they are going through.

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:01.967
And I definitely --
I think this book is --

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:05.070
as far as my novels go --
is the book that is most,

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:09.142
I think, informed
by my experience.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:10.843
-After a day where you feel

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:13.979
as though you've done
very good writing,

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:15.982
what do you do
at the end of it?

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.850
Like, when you finish --
when you like --

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.885
Do you write out longhand
or laptop? Is it...

00:05:19.885 --> 00:05:21.187
-Laptop?
-Alright. Laptop.

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:23.989
You close the laptop.
You're feeling good.

00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:26.558
What's the next thing you do?

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:30.696
-Uh...I...

00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:33.533
I do several --
There are several different ways

00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:36.302
that I waste time
and cut off my brain.

00:05:36.302 --> 00:05:38.671
-It's very -- I think that's
what we all want to hear,

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.407
is that you also waste time.
-Yes, yes.

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:44.543
-Sometimes when prolific writers
who have great skill come on,

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:46.212
we want to also know
that they will --

00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:48.014
"Yeah. I look at my phone."

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.649
-Right, right, right.
-So thank you.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:52.684
You're giving us great relief
by saying that, as well.

00:05:52.684 --> 00:05:54.586
-Good.
-Also I want to,

00:05:54.586 --> 00:05:57.055
before we let you go,
point out if people missed it.

00:05:57.055 --> 00:05:58.590
This is very exciting.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.761
This was just named
an Oprah Book Club pick.

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:03.962
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:06.466
And, uh...

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:09.502
And once again, I'm --
Nothing makes me happier

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:12.605
than when my tastes
are in line with Oprah.

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:15.274
And I think we both
have excellent taste.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.509
And it's really just
a wonderful, wonderful book.

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:19.845
And it's so happy --
I'm so happy to have you back.

00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:21.714
Thank you so much, Jesmyn.
-Thank you.

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:24.783
-You guys, that's Jesmyn Ward.

00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:26.051
"Let Us Descend"
is available now

00:06:26.051 --> 00:06:27.720
wherever you buy your books,
but please support

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.021
local and independent
booksellers.

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:30.890
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

